  "captions": {
    "fig4d_memory_layout": "Figure 4d: How the data is laid out and touched matters as much as the arithmetic: a fancy-index copy is 3,012x slower than a slice view, an einsum that bypasses BLAS 75x slower than Q @ X.T, gathering candidate rows before re-ranking 37x slower than reading them in place, and float64 2.1x slower than float32; row-major vs column-major access costs only 1.0x here because the 59 MB corpus fits in the 96 MB L3 cache.",
    "fig4_vectorisation": "Figure 4: Compiling the same loops with Numba gives 46x over pure Python, prange 623x and prange+fastmath (SIMD) 3,465x, versus 2,530x for NumPy/BLAS. NumPy loses whenever its single-threaded normalisation temporaries dominate: for one query over 20,000 docs it needs 20.1 ms versus 0.30 ms for the fused Numba kernel; for a 200-query batch Numba fastmath is faster (Numba 20.2 ms vs NumPy 26.5 ms)."
